welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Professor David Cameron a professor  of political science at Yale and the  director of the program in European  Union studies at the Macmillan Center  professor Cameron teaches courses on  European politics and the European Union  he has written extensively about the  impact of trade openness on government  and with respect to the EU about the  initiative to complete the internal  market the operation of the European  monetary system and the enlargement of  the EU professor Cameron joins us today  to talk about the EU and the European  economics crisis welcome professor  Cameron Thank You Marilyn it's good to  be here so let's do a little crash  course in EU 101 what is the European  Union okay the European Union is a union  of states there are now 27 states it  began with France Germany Italy Belgium  Netherlands and Luxembourg backed in  1952 in the Coal and Steel Community and  over the years it's expanded its  functions and it's expanded its  membership so that now it's 27 states  and probably within a decade or so  there'll be 33 34 35 States so it's a  very large organization it's more than  an international organization it has a  dense array of institutions at the  supranational level of Parliament  Commission Council of Ministers so it's  a it's a unique entity mm-hmm like the  United States Europe is facing a pretty  bleak economic forecast how does what  the EU is facing similar or dissimilar  to what the United States is going  through it's facing the same crisis it's  a global crisis in a global financial  crisis  and it's having a very severe effect in  Europe there's a very large contraction  going on in Europe increase in  unemployment as here and the major  problem is the Europeans really haven't  as yet responded as aggressively to the  contraction as the American  administration or the British  administration of Prime Minister Brown  okay and in a letter you wrote appearing  in the Financial Times you state that  Europe needs a much larger stimulus how  much more do you think is needed and why  well in in November back in November the  European Commission which is the  technical civil service of the European  Union drafted a plan for a stimulus of  200 billion euros which would be about  one and a half percent of GDP now you  compare that with the Obama stimulus of  787 billion dollars which is about five  and a half percent of our gdp so the  European stimulus is about a third the  size of ours most of it would come from  the member states themselves because the  EU doesn't really have very much fiscal  capacity now that was agreed in December  but i think it's the case and i think  most observers including the IMF the  oecd and the European Union staff itself  realized that the contraction is getting  worse and worse and the Europeans have  been reluctant to go any further than  they have gone as of December so they  are in a situation where they are not  doing what they could be doing and what  I think they should be doing and but I'm  just one voice of many who have been  saying this the Obama administration and  the IMF have been arguing for a much  larger stimulus in the  in the EU and there is a great deal of  reluctance unfortunately to do that the  cost is the contraction will go on  longer it'll be deeper they'll be later  to recover and the unemployment rate  will be higher as a result why do you  think they rejected the greater stimulus  well there was a good deal of debate in  the g20 the g20 the group of 20 is a  group of states countries that gathered  together the g7 which is the US and  canada and italy in germany and britain  and france and japan and then russia  added on as the g8 you add in a few more  countries you get to the g20 it includes  a number of developing or middle-income  countries China India Mexico Brazil for  example and the g20 met in London on  April second last week and had a serious  meeting dealing with the all aspects of  the crisis they could agree on the need  for the IMF to have a substantial  increase in its resources which could be  made available to countries having a  balance of payments crisis they could  agree at least in principle on  increasing financial regulation and  there's a perception in Europe that it  was hedge funds it was our financial  system let loose and running rampant  that contributed to this crisis so  there's a major push in Europe  especially for greater regulation what  they couldn't agree on was a larger  stimulus and the US had pushed that  quite hard in the meetings leading up to  the g20 summit meeting with the finance  ministers our treasury secretary had  been over there arguing for a stimulus  supported by IMF analyses and so forth  but the Europeans are very reluctant to  do that for variety of reasons  many of them really aren't canes Ian's  the continental Europeans don't really  buy the notion of canes counter-cyclical  policy they're much more reluctant to  run large budget deficits they have a  commitment in in the eurozone in  particular that is the 16 countries in  the EU that have the euro they have a  commitment not to incur what they call  excessive deficits which our deficits  over three percent of GDP you can't  really do an aggressive stimulus without  violating that rule in the eurozone and  the German government in particular is  very reluctant to support that any such  deficit so there was an agreement prior  to the g20 that the Americans wouldn't  push and and the issue was papered over  so basically nothing was done in terms  of a fiscal stimulus it was the great  from my point of view the great failure  of the g20 meeting that there wasn't a  conservative effort many of the  countries in the world are running very  substantial counter-cyclical policies  designed to pull the world and the  country out of the recession of China's  doing that Japan's doing that Britain's  doing that we're doing that  unfortunately the Europeans aren't and  who that hurts especially I think are  the some of the Europeans themselves in  particular the countries of Central and  Eastern Europe which depend on demand in  markets in Western Europe for the sale  of their goods and they're being hurt  and suffering greatly and several of the  country's Latvia hungry now Romania are  about to have to go two of them have  gone and Romania soon to go to the IMF  for substantial package of emergency  funding  which is a signal of the extent to which  their economies have deteriorated so the  issue is still on the table my sense is  the Europeans will meet in the European  Council which is the group of leaders of  heads of state and government of the EU  the 27 leaders will meet again in june  they met in March and decided not to do  anything they'll meet again in june to  assess their december recovery plan and  my suspicion is they will realize as the  oecd pointed out last week that the  contraction is much more severe than  people thought it would be it's getting  worse europe probably right now as of  the end of the first quarter has 16 or  17 or 18 countries in a recession  simultaneously we've never had such a  broad continent-wide synchronized  recession and my sense is the Europeans  will realize and come to the realization  that they have to do something so the  you painted a pretty bleak picture so  moving forward do you you know what do  you think will happen well I think the  Europeans will I think some of the  Europeans are doing a good job one of  the interesting things is that there are  divergent responses in the EU to the  crisis the Gordon Brown government has  been very aggressive has in some sense  set set the path for other countries in  how to respond it was the first first  substantial stimulus program came out of  Britain the first program to  recapitalize the banks came out of  Britain so Britain is responding  although it will nevertheless have an  unemployment rate around ten percent by  the end of this year some of the  Scandinavian member states are  responding some of the other countries  in Northern Europe such as Belgium and  the net  and Luxembourg are responding the real  foot draggers is I it seems to me our  France and Italy in Spain especially  France and Italy which could be doing  much more than they are doing right now  and my sense is they will decide and  realize they have to do more than what  they're doing there is an argument the  Europeans make and there's some validity  to it that because they have a much  larger welfare state at least some of  them do and a much larger scope of  spending by the government that they  have larger automatic stabilizers which  are essentially the automatic  counter-cyclical aspects of fiscal  policy revenues dropping at in a time of  contraction and expenditures increasing  in a time of contraction without the  government doing anything just happens  automatically so they're right to some  extent the European countries do have  larger automatic stabilizers but they're  a small portion of what what is needed  so they really do need a more  substantial commitment in a range of one  or two or three percentage points of GDP  and fiscal stimulus which means  basically increasing the size of the  deficit or reducing the size of the  budget surplus in those country few  countries that have a surplus so my  guess is they will get their act  together but it takes some time with 27  countries I mean one example the  Hungarian government which is under an  IMF loan package we requested last fall  or this winter a very substantial aid  program for Central and Eastern Europe  something in the range of 175 billion  euros for the Central and Eastern  European economies the Polish government  immediately said no thank you we don't  need that we're doing fine  and the Czech government did as well  there are problems in the EU because of  its size its diversity that makes it  very difficult for them to agree on  anything and it takes longer for them to  do anything then say an American  government or British government and  that's part of the reason so  unfortunately the Europeans will suffer  as a result well hopefully things will  turn around sooner rather than later  thank you very much for being with us  here today and sharing some of your  thoughts on the EU and the ongoing  financial crisis thank you for more  information about professor Cameron and  his work please visit our website at  yale edu backslash macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

